8  population and on the right you see the  tumor growth graph where you can see  that the tumors that were treated with  untyped pd-1 um have no response to the  treatment so we have a set up uh a  melanoma model set up we're  investigating are are therapeutic  and so here initially what you can see  is that when we treat uh melanoma tumors  this chlorine 24 with a well-type  vaccinia virus you are starting to see  some responses for the tumor you see a  partial respond of about  42  and  so uh we wanted to see what was actually  the makeup of the uh infiltration um of  these tumors after treatment  and as you can see here from single cell  analysis we can start seeing how are  these lymphocytes um distributed in the  tumor and one of the important things  that here to highlight is how in the pbs  group alone you have very little t cell  infiltration as you can see in the by  the pie charts about only like seven  percent there's quite a bit of  macrophages and monocytes um and then  particularly when you treat the tumors  with the vaccinia virus there's an  expansion of t-cell  the t-cell population there's also an  expansion of monocytes and a decrease in  the overall micro macrophage population  so this gives you an overview of how the  vaccinia virus affects the tumor the  tumor infiltrates  after treatment  and so looking a little bit more  specifically at the tumor infiltration  by flow cytometry  what we can find  by full cytometry as well is that  compared to the lymph node where you see  no  no changes in cda populations with the  vaccinia treatment which is also  very nice to see that there's no effect  into the lymph nodes you see that in the  till there's a dramatic increase of the  cda population after the vaccinia  treatment and  also important to highlight is that you  see a decrease of the phospho3-positive  t-rex population after the vaccemia  treatment and this is something that i  will touch on um towards the end of the  presentation  another important um  characteristic that we found in the cda  population of these tumors was that  there was a deficiency in their  metabolic state where you can see here  by twin btg where that shows glucose  uptake and myotracker for mitochondrial  content we see an overall decrease on  these two markers showing that there are  metabolic deficiencies in the t-cell  infiltration  and so  the idea now moving forward is how you  can you restore that metabolic function  of the t cells in the tumor  microenvironment you got the t-cells  into the tumor by using the oncolytic  now you need to overcome the different  barriers they encounter in the tumor and  so our lab has been able to do this in a  couple of different ways throughout the  years and as you can see here from this  uh text  we've done this by inducing pc and alpha  expression which promotes mitochondrial  biogenesis  we've done this by reducing oxygen  consumption from tumors uh through the  treatment of metformin allowing t cells  to use oxolof  um and we've done this also by using  41bp agonist where we know that it  promotes mitochondrial function and uh  consequently improving checkpoint okay  and so we were looking at other  metabolic  players that can also help us improve t  cell function and we decided to um  look a little bit into uh  pro-inflammatory cytokines and what we  found was that from the literature is  that leptin um  can also push t cells towards a more  oxidative  state where have been studies that shown  that they can stimulate fatty acid  oxidation as well as to as well as drive  a glucose to fatty acid um functions and  so this would give us an idea that  leptin could be uh  an interesting player and most  importantly that i'm not showing here we  do see that uh exhausted t cells can  express highly the leptin receptor  and so to do our initial studies  we did a  cda t cell culture in vitro where we  treat the t cells with different  concentrations of leptin and what we  find is that through metabolic analysis  of seahorse we show that leptin can  improve the oxygen consumption's rate as  you see in the top uh row of graphs  there's a higher okar which is oxygen  consumption rates and basal state as  well as higher spare respiratory  capacity and we can also see that there  is no major changes in terms of  glycolytic capacity  shown by the ecar  plot  and then when we look specifically at by  flow cytometry and mitochondrial content  in glucose uptake we do also see that by  leptin treatment you have an increase in  the mitochondrial mass staining  showing that leptin can actually have a  pro-oxidative  effect on t cells  and so how can we test the the effects  of leptin directly in the tumor  microenvironment the way that we uh came  about to do this experiments we decided  to take our melanoma model and uh  transduce them with a leptin gene where  we can see if the tumor will produce  leptin release it into the  microenvironment and this is exactly  what we find when the leptin is  expressed by a tumor they actually  release it into the tumor  microenvironment  and when you put these tumors into mice  um and follow their tumor growth we find  that the tumors that explode that are  expressing leptin are have a much  controlled tumor growth compared to  their control counterparts and also  increase the overall  survival of the mice  when we look at the tumor infiltrating  lymphocytes directly we do also see  similar to the  oncolytic treatments we see an increase  in the infiltration of cd8 as well as  the infiltration of cd4 t cells  most importantly we wanted to assess if  this effect of leptin uh in the on the  tumor growth was directly  directly correlated to the effects of  the cda t cells in the tumor so the way  that we did this was to  deplete the tumor the mouse from  cdas through it using an antibody and  what you find is that when you have a  tumor that is expressing leptin but you  depleted the cd8 from the tumor you no  longer have that control tumor growth  and these tumors start growing the same  as controls showing that is this effect  of tumor growth is dependent on cd8  function  more uh and further we were able to show  that this tumor  control growth was also dependent on the  expression of the leptin receptor  on  t cells where in this experiment we have  leptin receptor flux mice with a cd4  cree and we see that the tumors  the tumors that were expressed in leptin  that were  they were injected into the leptin  receptor knockouts also have a they lose  that a controlled tumor response showing  overall that the effects that we're  seeing in tumor growth are dependent on  the tumor infiltrating lymphocytes and  these lymphocytes being able to express  the leptin receptor  and so what is the functional state of  these tumor infiltrating lymphocytes  we find that these tumor infiltrating  lymphocytes when they are expressing  leptin they have a better functional  capacity as you can see here by  sustaining by looking at the expression  of interference gamma and tnf alpha  and as well that we we are observing an  improvement in mito and mitochondrial  function where we're seeing a higher  mitochondrial mass  in the tumors that are expressing leptin  and so  moving forward the idea is to be able to  deliver  the leptin directly into the tumor  microenvironment and the way that we did  this was by  cloning the leptin uh gene into a  vaccinia viral  expressing virus and we in that way what  we do is that we deliver the vaccinia to  the tumor the tumor will  the virus will release the leptin into  the tumor microenvironment and this will  improve the t cell function once they  get into the tumor  and when you we're looking at the  therapeutic responses of this vaccinia  virus what we see  is that the chlor24 melanoma model we  see a much better response with the  virus that expresses leptin as you can  see here there's a complete response  of about 27  in the melanoma model um we also  included a pancreatic cancer model  pancos2 where you don't see a complete  response but you definitely see a much  better partial response with the leptin  expressing virus um compared to the  virus control  when we're looking at the tumor  infiltrating lymphocytes we also see  that these tumors have a much better  functional t cell infiltration where you  can see that they're expressing much  more  uh cytokines in terms of tnf alpha and  in the pheno gamma compared to the  control virus  they're also producing they're also  generating  a lot more mitochondrial mass and this  is observed by the staining of vdac  you're seeing a lot more vdac a higher  percentage of vdac in the leptin  expressing virus tumors compared to  their controls and uh importantly too we  are actually observing an increase in uh  proliferative capacity in these t cells  as well where we see a higher percentage  of ti-67 staining  in the t cells from the  tumors  treated with the leptin vaccinia tumor  vaccinia virus  and so just to look a little bit further  at the populations that are in in the  tumor  after treatment and looking specifically  at the populations in terms of like the  t cell clusters we can divide the t  cells that are infiltrating into these  tumors after treatment in different  populations such as memory cytotoxic  c4t cells t-rex um exhausted and one of  the things that it was highlighted  during the analysis was that there was  an increase in general of the memory  population  and so when we did uh till analysis we  did see that the tumors that were  treated with the vaccine leptin have an  increase  in cd127 expressing tumor t cells  showing that in general there's a um and  a skewing towards a memory population  which would be also beneficial for  better uh response to the immunotherapy  so doing immediate so trying to see if  this memory activity is true  what we did was a memory experiment  where  we had a cohort of mice that got treated  with the vaccinia leptin expressing  virus  as